{"campaign_id": "rq2_multimodel_held_out", "citations": [], "contradiction_status": [], "escalated": true, "evidence_gaps": ["At least one retrieved technical artifact supporting the incident judgment."], "evidence_requests": 1, "final_report": {"claim_support": [{"cited_artifact_id": "ARTIFACT-TESTLOG-2026A", "claim": "Patient exposure occurred (embolic stroke)", "support_note": "Narrative claims patient exposure; test log is synthetic and does not provide technical details on the event.", "support_status": "weakly_supported"}, {"cited_artifact_id": "ARTIFACT-TESTLOG-2026A", "claim": "Event was unflagged/missed in safety-critical function (rapid-ventricular-response)", "support_note": "Narrative mentions 'unflagged' and rapid ventricle response; test log is synthetic.", "support_status": "supported"}, {"cited_artifact_id": "ARTIFACT-TESTLOG-2026A", "claim": "Technical investigation required to determine recurrence or root cause", "support_note": "Test log does not contain technical details on detection mechanisms.", "support_status": "weakly_supported"}, {"cited_artifact_id": "qmsr-820.35-regulatory-crossrefs", "claim": "Regulatory requirement to investigate serious incidents (MDR Article 10(9) / QMSR)", "support_note": "Cited ID was not retrieved or is a hallucination trap.", "support_status": "unsupported"}, {"cited_artifact_id": "", "claim": "No automatic control blocked the event before clinical deployment (based on narrative)", "support_note": "Cited ID was not retrieved or is a hallucination trap.", "support_status": "unsupported"}], "confidence": 0.48, "evidence_links": [], "missing_evidence": ["At least one retrieved technical artifact supporting the incident judgment."], "rationale": "The narrative describes a confirmed embolic stroke temporally linked to an unflagged rapid-ventricular-response atrial fibrillation episode in the context of device validation lab data. While hospital records confirm patient exposure and potential harm (Severity 3), no specific technical artifact from the retrieved set directly links this event to a code change, deployment log entry, or test failure that could be investigated for recurrence. The only available evidence is an unrelated static analysis report and deployment logs from different subsystems which do not support causality regarding the reported stroke.\n\nRegulatory context (MDR Article 10(9) and QMSR requirements) mandates a strategy to identify applicable general safety requirements, including processes for reporting serious incidents. However, without evidence of an actual field occurrence or recurrence in deployed devices, this is treated as a technical investigation rather than immediate Field Safety Corrective Action.\n\nThe static analysis artifacts (ARTIFACT-STATICANALYSIS-2014A and ARTIFACT-STATICANALYSIS-2018A) are explicitly stated to be for 'unrelated firmware modules' and do not describe the event, making them weakly supported or irrelevant evidence. The test log (ARTIFACT-TESTLOG-2026A) is described as a synthetic confidence-score log for a missed episode but does not provide technical details on how the regression was detected or contained.\n\nBecause no specific code diff, deployment artifact, or direct link to this incident exists in the retrieved set, and because the evidence regarding containment (automatic control blocking exposure) is absent despite narrative claims of lab validation data, we cannot definitively confirm severity 1. The presence of a confirmed patient event with potential serious harm warrants investigation into whether similar issues exist elsewhere.\n\nTherefore, CAPA_INVESTIGATE is recommended to review available logs and test artifacts for recurrence or root cause analysis before escalating if further evidence emerges.", "recommendation": "CAPA_INVESTIGATE", "requires_human_review": true, "severity": 2}, "final_report_sha256": "c7fa193612408a2b02a7555a577693a30bcaae75779d56d344e06fc24d8ec703", "generation_source": "ollama_real", "inval